    "body": "Westinghouse Electric Company is investing approximately $1 billion in a new nuclear fuel factory in Hopkins, South Carolina, with construction underway and production expected to begin in 2030. The building will sit alongside the company’s existing Columbia Fuel Fabrication Facility in Richland County.\nWhat’s Related\nThe factory will produce low-enriched uranium plus (LEU+) for nuclear power plants. Westinghouse says the fuel can help reactors generate more electricity and run longer between refuelings, reducing the time they must shut down to replace fuel.\nThe investment will bring commercial-scale production of the fuel to the Columbia site, serving existing reactors and future Westinghouse AP1000 reactors worldwide. The company describes the project as the first new fleet-scale nuclear fuel factory built in the United States in more than 50 years.\nSouth Carolina’s Coordinating Council for Economic Development approved job development credits for the expansion , although the state did not disclose their value.\nA different manufacturing process\nWestinghouse says the existing plant’s layout and manufacturing technology cannot accommodate the planned commercial production of higher-enrichment fuel. The expansion will place that work in a purpose-built facility next to the current plant.\nThe building will use more automation and a “dry” manufacturing process similar to one already used at Westinghouse’s factory in the United Kingdom. The company says the process will reduce chemical use and lower the potential for environmental impacts.\nThe Columbia facility, built in 1968, has more than 1,200 employees. It produces fuel pellets, rods, assemblies, and components for customers around the world and other Westinghouse manufacturing plants.\n“A strong American nuclear industry starts with a secure, domestic supply of nuclear fuel,” said Ted Garrish, assistant secretary for nuclear energy.\nConstruction, suppliers, and hiring\nWestinghouse expects several hundred contractors to work on the project at its peak, alongside an internal team of approximately 30 to 50 people. The company says the construction will create opportunities for local contractors, subcontractors and suppliers.\nSome of the new manufacturing technology will require additional technical skills. Westinghouse says it is working with local technical schools, colleges, and universities to help prepare employees for that work.\nA study commissioned by Westinghouse and conducted with researchers at the University of South Carolina’s Darla Moore School of Business projects $4.9 billion in statewide economic impact over 10 years, including approximately $3.8 billion in Richland County.\nThe operating benefit Westinghouse is pursuing centers on keeping reactors running longer between fuel changes. Discussing the initial deployment in 2025, Westinghouse Nuclear Fuel President Tarik Choho said: “LEU+ fuel is a perfect example of how we can help reduce the number of outages in nuclear power plants.”",
